{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.1.1 | 05/24/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Fixed {\b Help → About} — was opening the upstream maintainer's website. Now opens an in-app dialog showing the current version, fork attribution, and links to the fork's repo, issues page, upstream site, and dalaya.org. The system tray "About" entry routes to the same dialog.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Fixed {\b Help → Release Notes} — was opening the upstream maintainer's web page. Now opens an in-app window that renders the bundled release notes file directly. Read-only and scrollable; no external app dependency. Shows only fork-specific releases (upstream entries dropped since the fork has diverged).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Fixed {\b auto pet detection}. The {\b spells.txt} target-type column was previously hardcoded to {\b Single Target} for every spell, so the parser's check for pet-only buffs never matched and unknown pets had to be added via {\b petmapping.txt} or the {\b Pet Owners} window. The conversion script now reads the real target type from Dalaya's {\b spells_us.txt} (column 98), and 118 pet-targeted spells in the regenerated database correctly flag their recipients as pets. Pets receiving Magician/Beastlord/Necromancer pet buffs will be added to the registry automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab Fixed {\b diagnostic log file location}. Log4net was writing {\b EQLogParser.log} to {\b %AppData%\\EQLogParser\\logs\\} (the upstream folder) instead of {\b %AppData%\\EQLogParser-Dalaya\\logs\\} (the fork's data folder). New log entries now go to the correct location. Old log files in the upstream folder are orphaned but harmless to delete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab Window title and Task Manager entry now show {\b EQLogParser-Dalaya} instead of {\b EQLogParser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 6.\tx360\tab Fixed {\b healing parser} for Dalaya. The upstream parser only recognized {\b "<Healer> healed <Target> for N hit points by <Spell>"} style heal lines, so on Dalaya — which uses {\b "Your <Spell> healed <Target> for N damage."} and {\b "<Healer> performs an exceptional heal! (N)"} for crit announcements — roughly 94% of heal events were silently dropped. The parser now captures Dalaya's heal patterns and pairs exceptional-heal announcements to their observable heal records as crit modifiers. Heal totals and crit annotations on the {\b DPS Summary} / {\b Tanking Summary} healing views now reflect actual heal activity instead of showing single-event totals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 7.\tx360\tab {\b DPS Summary} column drag is now disabled to match the other grids.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.1.0 | 05/13/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab New {\b Raid Damage} view (under {\b View} menu) combines multiple raiders' logs into one merged DPS parse. Add each raider's eqlog file as a source; the merger auto-detects timezone offsets, compensates for clock drift within a fight, deduplicates events seen by multiple observers via order-pair matching, and filters damage-shield records to the holder's own log so they're not double-counted. The merged view embeds the same {\b DPS Summary} and {\b Tanking Summary} controls as the main tabs (column chooser, pet rollups, right-click breakdowns). Each source's "you/your" lines resolve to that source's player, so self-cast spell damage attributes correctly across observers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Cross-timezone accuracy: same-hit records that disagreed on crit/lucky/twincast annotation across sources (because the 1-second pairing window in {\b DamageLineParser} fires per-client and intra-second log line ordering varies) were splitting into separate dedup buckets and double-counting. The merger now treats the unparsed {\b ModifiersMask} sentinel as a wildcard and OR's modifier bits across sources. Verified end-to-end with a 4-source / 4-fight Dalaya raid sample — Iskkath's Experiment, Kara'Kadar, Old Overgrowth, and Skoal the Malignant now parse within ~1% of dev-stated boss HP (vs 7–20% over before the fix).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Multi-file per player: a single raider can supply multiple log clips (e.g. separate exports per boss) and they get parsed into the same source row. Duplicate prevention keys on file path, not player name. Status text reads {\b "(N files: ...)"} when more than one is loaded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab New {\b Help → "Log Merge Diagnostics (Raid Damage)"} toggle. When enabled, the merger writes {\b [MergeDiag]} lines to {\b EQLogParser.log} identifying any dedup-key splits and the field that caused them. Off by default; only needed when investigating a suspected cross-source over-count.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.0.8 | 05/05/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Restored {\b crit detection} for Dalaya's melee and wizard direct-damage crits. Dalaya logs critical hits as a separate announcement line ({\b "X scores a critical hit! (N)"}, {\b "X delivers a critical blast! (N)"}, {\b "X lands a Crippling Blow!(N)"}, or {\b "X scores a Finishing Blow!!"}) followed by the actual damage line. The handlers that pair these two lines were inadvertently dropped in 1.0.7 alongside unrelated EMU-only code; this release restores them as always-on Dalaya behavior. Crit %, Crit Total, and Lucky/Crit breakdowns in {\b DPS Summary} will now populate correctly for melee classes and wizard direct-damage spells. Aggregate damage totals are unaffected — only the crit annotation on those records changes.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.0.7 | 05/03/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Merged upstream {\b EQLogParser 2.3.51}. The two notable user-facing pickups are {\b Group View} on the {\b Damage Summary} (assigns players to groups using /who all raid output for combined-DPS rollups; switch via the existing {\b Player +Pets} dropdown) and a new {\b Best Sec} column on the {\b Tank Summary}. The merge also brings improved automatic pet detection and a large amount of internal code reorganization. Some minor UI regressions are possible during the bedding-in period; please report anything that looks wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Removed the {\b Use EMU Server Parsing} option from the {\b Options} menu. Dalaya is itself an EMU server, so the toggle was never meaningful for this fork — it had to be left off, and the EMU-only parsing branches it controlled were dead code. Removing the toggle simplifies the parser and eliminates a configuration footgun. This change is invisible to users who never touched the toggle (the great majority).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.0.6 | 05/03/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Updated the {\b spells database} to match the latest Dalaya patch. 56 new spells were added, 4 spells were renamed by the patch (e.g. {\b Intensify} to {\b Barrier of the Gruplok}, {\b Fire Damage IX} to {\b Draconic Hellfire}), and lands-on / wear-off messages were corrected for 19 existing spells. Also restored the full text of {\b Wave of Revulsion}'s landing message, which was previously truncated mid-word. The rebuild process is now scripted ({\b tools/spells/convert_spells.py}) so future Dalaya patches can be picked up in a single command.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.0.5 | 04/29/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Added a {\b Previous Line Window (s)} option to {\b Match Previous Line} on triggers. Previously the previous-line check only inspected the log line directly before the current one, which made cast-to-spell-land triggers fragile in busy raids where intervening lines break the strict adjacency. Setting the window to a non-zero number of seconds lets the trigger scan that far back and fire on the most recent matching line within the window. This is the right way to bind a spell cast to its later landing or damage line so a DoT timer doesn't start on a resist or fizzle. Default of 0 preserves the existing immediate-prior-line behavior, so existing triggers are unchanged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Restored {\b End Early}, {\b Timer Warning}, and {\b Reset Duration} options on {\b Fast Countdown} timers. The trigger runtime always supported these; only the property editor was hiding them when Timer Type was set to Fast Countdown. End-early patterns and pre-end warnings now work the same way for Fast Countdown as they do for the standard Countdown mode.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Trigger {\b Duration} now accepts fractional seconds. Below 60 seconds the field shows decimal seconds (e.g. {\b 3.3} or {\b 59.9}); from 60 seconds upward it shows {\b hh:mm:ss} as before. Existing whole-second timers are unchanged. Short-duration Countdown timers also use the faster overlay render cadence so the progress bar updates smoothly at sub-second durations. Note: the timer overlay rounds remaining time to whole seconds unless {\b Show Milliseconds} is enabled on the overlay, so consider toggling that on for overlays that host sub-second triggers.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.0.3 | 04/24/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Added two variants to the {\b Fight List} right-click menu for exporting selected fights: {\b Export Selected Fights (with adds)} keeps the existing behavior (writes every combat line in the selected fights' time window, including ambient adds, trash, and wand procs), and {\b Export Selected Fights (without adds)} filters to only the selected fight names — dropping unrelated NPCs that overlapped in time. The "without adds" variant is the right choice for sharing exports across multiple players' logs: each imported file contributes exactly the fights selected, with no phantom fights from overlapping encounters. Single-parse use cases still benefit from the original "with adds" behavior.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.0.2 | 04/23/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Fixed duplicate named-pet rows in {\b DPS Summary}. Some non-melee direct-damage lines (e.g. {\b Blood Fountain} procs from Bonaparte) were still leaving a trailing space on the attacker name, so the same pet appeared as two rows in the DPS breakdown. The pet now correctly rolls up as a single row under the owning player.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Fixed a phantom DPS row that showed only a time value and no damage. Pet miss lines such as {\b "Bonaparte  tries to bash NPC, but NPC is INVULNERABLE!"} were registering a separate attacker entry because of the trailing space on the pet name, producing an empty row alongside the real one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Fixed {\b pet owner mis-attribution} in raid logs. The parser was assigning every named pet it saw (including other players' pets like {\b Fireballs} or {\b Otto}) to the character whose log was being parsed, overwriting correct mappings in {\b petmapping.txt}. Pets are now marked as pets but ownership is only set from {\b petmapping.txt}, the pet's own {\b "My leader is X"} announcement, or the {\b Pet Owners} window.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.0.1 | 04/21/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Fixed pet DPS attribution. Named pet attacker names are now trimmed of their trailing space at the point of detection so damage records store the clean name. Previously the trailing space caused pet DPS lookups to miss, keeping named pets as separate rows in DPS Summary instead of rolling up under their owner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Added support for {\b Dalaya's damage shield} lines ({\b "X was hit by non-melee for Y points of damage"}). The parser now defers attribution until the immediately following melee hit identifies the shielded player. DS damage is now correctly credited to the player wearing the shield rather than being dropped.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Fixed duplicate pet entries in the {\b Pet Owners} window. Pet names are now trimmed at the point of entry in {\b AddPetToPlayer} and {\b AddVerifiedPet}, with a safety-net trim in {\b GetPlayerFromPet} and {\b IsVerifiedPet} as well.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs24 1.0.0 | 04/21/26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Initial release of {\b EQLogParser-Dalaya}, a fork of {\b EQLogParser} by kauffman12 (v2.3.50), customized for the {\b Dalaya} EverQuest emulation server.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Rewrote {\b damage line parsing} to support Dalaya's log format. Dalaya uses {\b "X has taken N damage from your SpellName"} for player spells and {\b "X has taken N damage from AttackerName by SpellName"} for group/pet spells, which is the reverse of the live EQ format.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Added correct attribution of {\b player DoT and DD spell damage} to the casting player. Previously all spell damage was misattributed to a phantom entity or shown as separate DPS rows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab Added support for {\b named pets} (e.g. Bonaparte, Fireballs, Otto). Dalaya logs named pets with a double-space after their name, which is now detected and used to reliably identify them as pets and link them to their owner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab Fixed {\b pet spell damage} attribution. Spells cast by named pets such as Blood Fountain and Caustic Mist are now correctly shown under the pet owner's DPS rather than as a separate player.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 6.\tx360\tab Fixed {\b other players' spell damage} attribution. Spells cast by group and raid members (e.g. {\b "from Puckin by Ancient: Fatebind"}) are now correctly identified and attributed to the casting player.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 7.\tx360\tab Replaced the {\b spells.txt} database with one generated from Dalaya's {\b spells_us.txt}. This ensures spell names, landing messages, and spell classifications reflect Dalaya's custom spells rather than live EQ spells which share IDs but have different names and effects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 8.\tx360\tab Fixed the {\b Damaging} field in the spells database so that {\b GetDamagingSpellByName} correctly identifies Dalaya spells, enabling accurate DoT vs DD classification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 9.\tx360\tab Fixed {\b pet ownership persistence}. The {\b PlayerManager.Save()} method previously excluded named pets with trailing spaces from being written to {\b petmapping.txt} due to a name validation check. Pet-to-owner mappings now survive application restarts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs20 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 10.\tx360\tab Disabled {\b EMU Parsing mode} requirement for Dalaya. Dalaya uses a newer log format that does not require the legacy EMU parsing toggle, which was causing spell and damage lines to be swapped incorrectly when enabled.\sa180\par}
}